    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lift actually works? specifically how bernoulli's principle and newtons third law both play a part in it. i get the general idea but i want to understand how they work together.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'd be happy to break down the mechanics of lift. Bernoulli's principle states that as air moves faster, its pressure decreases, which leads to lift when air flows over a curved surface, like an airplane wing. Newton's third law, also known as action-reaction, comes into play when the air molecules strike the curved surface, exerting a force upward on the wing. Simultaneously, the wing exerts an equal force downward on the air molecules, creating an upward force called lift. This combination of Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law creates an upward force, resulting in the lifting motion of an airplane.",
      "source": "target",
